# CLAUDE.md
This file is Claude Code's standing instruction set for this project. Claude
Code reads it at the start of every session and treats every rule in it as in
force until the file changes. Edits to this file are governed by /bmad-wrap's
CLAUDE.md gate: a zero-additions-by-default discipline with a four-check
approval path for the rare additions that pass the gate.

## Projects and Environment
This project lives at the working directory the user opened Claude Code in.
The repository root is the directory containing this CLAUDE.md file.
Subdirectories under the project root that hold their own CLAUDE.md files
extend the rules below with module-specific instructions; the most specific
file wins in a conflict, and a rule in this file is the project-wide default.
The user's preferred working directories outside this project are not your
concern unless the user names them in a turn.

## BMAD Workflow
This project uses Keelswell for non-trivial work. Two workflows are in scope:
- Wave-based development is the default for any work item the user calls an
  epic, a wave, a story, or anything connected to /bmad-create-wave or
  /bmad-dev-wave. When the user names that kind of work, dispatch via the
  Keelswell skills under .claude/skills/; do not free-hand the orchestration.
- Quick-dev is for one-off, narrow, single-file changes the user calls a fix,
  a typo, a small refactor, or a one-line config tweak. Quick-dev bypasses
  the BMAD wave skills and uses direct edits.
If the user's request does not name a mode and the work could be either, ask
one clarifying question before starting.

## Communication Style
Address the user as {user_name} in chat output.
Cite files by file:line when referring to specific code, in the form
path/to/file.ext:lineNumber; the line number is the line of the cited
identifier.
Do not use em-dashes (U+2014) or en-dashes (U+2013) in any output, ever.
Substitute " -- " (space-hyphen-hyphen-space) where the prose needs a dash;
restructure sentences when " -- " reads awkwardly. The em-dash scrub rule
below enforces this with a mechanical check.
Do not use emojis in any output, ever.
Prefer prose to bullets when the content is conceptual; use bullets only for
genuine lists of discrete items; avoid bullets of one or two items.

## Working With Me
Ask clarifying questions one at a time; do not batch questions. Do not write
code until every clarifying question is resolved. When a request is
ambiguous, your first turn is either an answer to a question the user asked
or a clarifying question of your own; do not assume, guess, or silently pick
an interpretation. If the user tells you to just proceed, proceed -- the
clarifying-question discipline is the default, not a veto.

## Coding Discipline
Touch only what the request requires; the diff should be the smallest set of
modifications that accomplishes the named task. Do not improve adjacent code;
surface refactoring opportunities as separate questions. Clean up only your
own mess: remove files you introduced that are no longer needed, and leave a
prior developer's dead files alone unless asked. Surface dead code, do not
silently fix it: report unused functions, never-read parameters, and
unreachable branches with file:line references and let the user decide.

## Em-Dash Scrub
After every edit, before reporting completion to the user, run the em-dash
scrub on the change's diff:
    git diff | grep '^+' | grep -c $'\xe2\x80\x94'
The count must be zero (grep -c prints 0 and exits non-zero when there are no
matches; zero is the passing value). The same check runs mechanically in the
.claude/hooks/em-dash-scrub.sh hook; this rule exists so the em-dash never
reaches the hook.

## Bulk-Edit Triage
If a change touches ten or more files in a single edit, produce a triage
table before applying any edit, and present the table to the user. The table
has three columns: File (the path), Change (a phrase), Reason (a phrase tying
the change to the request). Apply the edits only after the user approves the
table; correct any disputed row first.

## Skip Skill When Discipline Is Absorbed
The first invocation of a Keelswell skill in a session reads the skill's
SKILL.md in full. The second invocation of the same skill in the same session
may skip the re-read if the discipline is internalized -- the steps, gates,
and outputs are clear. The heuristic: if running the skill from memory would
produce the same on-disk artifacts as reading it first, skip the re-read; if
there is any doubt about a specific step, re-read.

## Read Tool Source Before Running
For any non-trivial tool or script the project introduces -- a build helper,
a test runner, a custom CLI, an MCP server -- read the source of the tool
before invoking it. Closure prose, README files, and inline help can lag
behind the implementation; the source is the truth. Exceptions: widely
standardized tools (git, grep, ls, find, cargo, npm, pytest). For
project-local tools and any tool the user introduces in the session, read the
source first.

## Code Intelligence (LSP-First)
Prefer LSP-driven navigation and refactoring over text-pattern grep when an
LSP is available for the project's languages: go-to-definition,
find-references, rename-symbol. Fall back to grep only when the LSP returns
no results or the language has no integrated LSP. The LSP is structurally
aware; grep is textually aware.

## Self-Check Criteria
At the end of every substantive turn, before reporting completion, verify:
1. The change touches only files the user's request mentioned, or files
   clearly required by the request.
2. No em-dashes were introduced (em-dash scrub above).
3. No adjacent code was improved beyond the request's scope.
4. Any dead code encountered is surfaced, not silently removed.
5. The smallest viable test passes; if no test exists, the smallest manual
   verification step the change supports runs cleanly.
If any criterion fails, fix the issue silently and re-run the check. Report
completion only after all five pass.
